00:49:03.460 --> 00:49:05.090
more Cloud providers,
as I mentioned,

00:49:05.090 --> 00:49:06.940
have a government
Cloud option where

00:49:06.940 --> 00:49:08.830
the servers are
completely isolated

00:49:08.830 --> 00:49:10.550
from the public infrastructure.

00:49:10.550 --> 00:49:13.030
So only government agencies
have access to this.

00:49:13.030 --> 00:49:15.070
So you might want to
utilize one of those

00:49:15.070 --> 00:49:16.480
if you're moving to the Cloud.

00:49:16.480 --> 00:49:19.300
And as far as, like
sharing data information,

00:49:19.300 --> 00:49:22.270
we've actually been
helping a client come up

00:49:22.270 --> 00:49:24.400
with a virtual
cleanroom scenario.

00:49:24.400 --> 00:49:27.100
So as you mentioned,
how can you keep

00:49:27.100 --> 00:49:29.320
how can you isolate
data and code and stuff

00:49:29.320 --> 00:49:30.918
like that from people?

00:49:30.918 --> 00:49:33.460
Because you don't want everybody
to have access to that data.

00:49:33.460 --> 00:49:35.890
You don't have access to
your proprietary code.

00:49:35.890 --> 00:49:39.010
And there's a lot of
privacy concerns there.

00:49:39.010 --> 00:49:42.310
And so you can use
something like carbonating

00:49:42.310 --> 00:49:45.520
to containerize your
solutions and windows

00:49:45.520 --> 00:49:48.490
containerize solutions configure
all the privacy requirements

00:49:48.490 --> 00:49:49.480
that you need.

00:49:49.480 --> 00:49:51.910
And if you can imagine
like a push button

00:49:51.910 --> 00:49:53.690
exercise where somebody
presses a button,

00:49:53.690 --> 00:49:56.260
and it automatically
launches a container

00:49:56.260 --> 00:49:59.680
that has all of the access
pre-configured and associated

00:49:59.680 --> 00:50:00.880
with their profile.

00:50:00.880 --> 00:50:02.990
And so that's what we
call a virtual cleanroom.

00:50:02.990 --> 00:50:05.110
So it essentially
means that everybody

00:50:05.110 --> 00:50:07.150
who's working off
this environment

00:50:07.150 --> 00:50:10.960
is completely they're getting
like a fresh, clean install,

00:50:10.960 --> 00:50:14.080
a completely unperturbed
by anybody else's sessions.

00:50:14.080 --> 00:50:16.520
And so it's completely
independent of everybody else.

00:50:16.520 --> 00:50:18.393
And it's only been used by them.

00:50:18.393 --> 00:50:19.810
And it only has
access to the data

00:50:19.810 --> 00:50:22.102
that their profile should
allow them to have access to.

00:50:22.102 --> 00:50:24.610
So that's one way we're
looking at helping clients

00:50:24.610 --> 00:50:25.240
at the moment.

00:50:25.240 --> 00:50:29.290
And one of the best practices
that I can see right now,

00:50:29.290 --> 00:50:31.130
our future best practices.

00:50:31.130 --> 00:50:33.960
So it's a really
exciting project.

